1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,384
In this video, we'll
learn partial derivatives,

2
00:00:03,384 --> 00:00:06,403
the chain rule and Taylor
series the easy way,

3
00:00:06,403 --> 00:00:11,391
and see how meteorology uses them
to follow a weather balloon's temperature,

4
00:00:11,391 --> 00:00:16,050
and to find how much more water vapour
it takes to saturate warmer air.

5
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:21,050
What are partial derivatives,
the chain rule and Taylor series?

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,875
Let's see what they are, and why
they're built this way.

7
00:00:25,225 --> 00:00:28,450
Then let's see what they
tell us about the weather.

8
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:33,275
Let's start with a quantity that
changes with just one thing.

9
00:00:33,625 --> 00:00:36,765
At Brest, a port
at the western tip of France,

10
00:00:36,765 --> 00:00:40,042
the weather station's
thermometer read ten point

11
00:00:40,042 --> 00:00:43,046
three degrees Celsius
at six in the evening,

12
00:00:43,046 --> 00:00:47,211
universal time, on the first
of November twenty twenty-three,

13
00:00:47,211 --> 00:00:49,600
as Storm Ciarán's warm air arrived.

14
00:00:49,950 --> 00:00:53,400
Two hours later, it read
fourteen point nine.

15
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:55,875
How fast did it warm?

16
00:00:56,225 --> 00:00:59,302
We take
the change, four point six degrees,

17
00:00:59,302 --> 00:01:01,950
and divide it by the time, two hours.

18
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:05,050
That's two point three degrees per hour.

19
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:09,300
But that's an average over two hours.

20
00:01:09,650 --> 00:01:13,000
The derivative is the rate at one instant.

21
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:17,625
To get the rate at one instant,
we shrink the time step.

22
00:01:17,975 --> 00:01:24,150
Let's see how, on an easy curve: f of x
equals x squared, at x equals three.

23
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:28,604
A step from three to four
raises f from nine to sixteen,

24
00:01:28,604 --> 00:01:31,975
so the rate is seven
over one, which is seven.

25
00:01:33,475 --> 00:01:36,357
A step of one tenth gives six point one,

26
00:01:36,357 --> 00:01:40,175
and a step of one hundredth
gives six point zero one.

27
00:01:41,675 --> 00:01:45,225
As the step shrinks,
the rate settles on six.

28
00:01:45,575 --> 00:01:49,800
That limit is the derivative,
written d f by d x.

29
00:01:50,150 --> 00:01:54,650
For x squared it's two x, which
is six at x equals three.

30
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:55,941
In the picture,

31
00:01:55,941 --> 00:01:59,955
the line through two points
of the curve turns into the tangent,

32
00:01:59,955 --> 00:02:02,275
the line that just touches the curve.

33
00:02:02,625 --> 00:02:05,924
So the derivative
is the slope of the tangent:

34
00:02:05,924 --> 00:02:08,650
how much f changes for each unit of x.

35
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:14,400
In the weather, though, a quantity
rarely depends on just one thing.

36
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:18,050
The temperature depends
on where you are, and when.

37
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:22,300
Let's call the distance
east x, the distance north y,

38
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,875
the height z, and the time small t.

39
00:02:25,225 --> 00:02:27,720
Then the temperature, capital T,

40
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:31,775
is a function of all four:
T of x, y, z and small t.

41
00:02:32,125 --> 00:02:37,450
A quantity that has a value at every
place and every time is called a field.

42
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:42,853
Here's the temperature field a little more
than a kilometre above western France,

43
00:02:42,853 --> 00:02:44,225
at six in the evening.

44
00:02:44,575 --> 00:02:50,266
It comes from ERA5, a reanalysis:
a best estimate of the whole atmosphere,

45
00:02:50,266 --> 00:02:53,650
built from observations
and a weather model.

46
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:58,225
So how fast does the temperature
change as we go east?

47
00:02:58,575 --> 00:03:01,200
Now four things could change at once.

48
00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:05,975
The trick is to change only one
of them, and hold the others fixed.

49
00:03:06,325 --> 00:03:08,518
Let's lift the map into a landscape,

50
00:03:08,518 --> 00:03:12,050
where the height of the ground
stands for the temperature.

51
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:15,975
Now cut it with a wall that
runs due east through Brest.

52
00:03:16,325 --> 00:03:22,000
Along that cut, only x changes, while
y, the height and the time stay fixed.

53
00:03:22,350 --> 00:03:25,238
The edge of the cut is an ordinary curve,

54
00:03:25,238 --> 00:03:29,675
and its slope is the partial
derivative of T with respect to x.

55
00:03:30,025 --> 00:03:35,200
We write it with a curly d, and say
partial capital T by partial x.

56
00:03:35,550 --> 00:03:40,250
Partial means that only x changes,
and everything else stays put.

57
00:03:41,050 --> 00:03:43,860
Working out a partial derivative is easy:

58
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:49,000
treat every other variable as a constant,
and take the ordinary derivative.

59
00:03:49,350 --> 00:03:52,904
Let's try an easy,
made-up temperature field:

60
00:03:52,904 --> 00:03:56,379
T equals ten, minus
zero point zero three x,

61
00:03:56,379 --> 00:04:01,275
plus three small t, with x in
kilometres and small t in hours.

62
00:04:01,625 --> 00:04:06,473
For partial capital T by
partial x, small t is a constant,

63
00:04:06,473 --> 00:04:10,150
so the three small t term
has no slope in x.

64
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:15,550
What's left is minus zero point zero three
degrees per kilometre.

65
00:04:17,050 --> 00:04:20,177
For partial capital T by partial small t,

66
00:04:20,177 --> 00:04:24,600
x is the constant, and we get
plus three degrees per hour.

67
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:30,525
Now a field with a product,
T equals x times small t.

68
00:04:30,875 --> 00:04:36,350
Holding small t fixed, partial
capital T by partial x is small t.

69
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:41,775
Holding x fixed, partial
capital T by partial small t is x.

70
00:04:42,575 --> 00:04:44,525
Now the real field.

71
00:04:44,875 --> 00:04:48,926
The ERA5 data come on a grid
every quarter of a degree,

72
00:04:48,926 --> 00:04:53,125
which here is about eighteen
kilometres apart going east.

73
00:04:53,475 --> 00:04:57,976
So to find the slope at Brest, we
take the grid point one step east and

74
00:04:57,976 --> 00:05:03,175
the point one step west, and divide their
difference by the distance between them.

75
00:05:03,525 --> 00:05:08,250
That's called a centred difference, and
we'll see later why it's a good choice.

76
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:14,166
East of Brest it was colder, so partial
capital T by partial x is negative:

77
00:05:14,166 --> 00:05:18,100
minus three point two degrees
per hundred kilometres.

78
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:24,052
Cutting north instead gives
partial capital T by partial y:

79
00:05:24,052 --> 00:05:27,975
minus two point five degrees
per hundred kilometres.

80
00:05:29,475 --> 00:05:34,600
So at that moment, the air got colder
towards the east, and towards the north.

81
00:05:34,950 --> 00:05:38,762
Together, these two partial
derivatives make the gradient,

82
00:05:38,762 --> 00:05:41,325
which has its own movie in this series.

83
00:05:42,125 --> 00:05:46,000
What about partial
capital T by partial small t?

84
00:05:46,350 --> 00:05:50,550
Now we hold the place
fixed, and let only the time change.

85
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:54,253
That's what a thermometer
fixed at one place measures,

86
00:05:54,253 --> 00:05:56,675
so let's call it the fixed thermometer.

87
00:05:57,025 --> 00:05:58,785
At that height over Brest,

88
00:05:58,785 --> 00:06:02,780
the reanalysis temperature rose
from two point four degrees

89
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:06,300
at five o'clock to eight point two
at seven o'clock.

90
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:08,185
Over those two hours,

91
00:06:08,185 --> 00:06:11,108
partial capital T by partial small t was

92
00:06:11,108 --> 00:06:14,250
about plus two point nine
degrees per hour.

93
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:21,525
At the ground, the station's barometer
shows another partial derivative in time.

94
00:06:21,875 --> 00:06:24,516
The pressure fell from nine hundred and

95
00:06:24,516 --> 00:06:29,458
eighty eight point three hectopascals
at five o'clock to nine hundred and

96
00:06:29,458 --> 00:06:31,625
eighty three point three at six.

97
00:06:31,975 --> 00:06:35,875
Hectopascals are the pressure
unit of weather maps.

98
00:06:36,225 --> 00:06:41,850
That's a fall of five hectopascals in one
hour, a sign of a deep storm coming.

99
00:06:43,350 --> 00:06:47,825
And partial capital T by
partial z, the change with height?

100
00:06:48,175 --> 00:06:50,575
For that we need a weather balloon.

101
00:06:50,925 --> 00:06:55,592
Once or twice a day, at about eight
hundred stations around the world,

102
00:06:55,592 --> 00:06:58,592
a balloon carries a small
instrument package,

103
00:06:58,592 --> 00:07:01,725
called a radiosonde,
up through the atmosphere.

104
00:07:02,075 --> 00:07:06,660
Let's take the latest one we have
from Trappes, near Paris: noon,

105
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:11,175
universal time, on the thirtieth
of September twenty twenty-six.

106
00:07:11,525 --> 00:07:16,225
At one and a half kilometres up, it
measured twelve point three degrees.

107
00:07:16,575 --> 00:07:21,425
At five point eight kilometres,
it measured minus eleven point eight.

108
00:07:21,775 --> 00:07:25,790
So partial capital T by partial z was
minus twenty four

109
00:07:25,790 --> 00:07:29,658
point one degrees over
four point two six kilometres,

110
00:07:29,658 --> 00:07:33,600
which is minus five point seven
degrees per kilometre.

111
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:38,933
The balloon took fifteen minutes
to climb between those heights,

112
00:07:38,933 --> 00:07:43,125
and we'll check later that the air
itself hardly changed in that time.

113
00:07:43,475 --> 00:07:47,357
Meteorologists call this cooling
with height the lapse rate,

114
00:07:47,357 --> 00:07:48,846
and they flip its sign,

115
00:07:48,846 --> 00:07:53,375
so here the lapse rate is plus
five point seven degrees per kilometre.

116
00:07:54,175 --> 00:07:58,650
Now let's look at the temperature over all
of northern France at once,

117
00:07:58,650 --> 00:07:59,864
at the same moment,

118
00:07:59,864 --> 00:08:04,850
on five levels from near the ground
to about sixteen and a half kilometres up.

119
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:09,766
Above Trappes, the change in temperature
divided by the gap in height

120
00:08:09,766 --> 00:08:13,191
between neighbouring levels
is minus six point two,

121
00:08:13,191 --> 00:08:14,601
minus five point six,

122
00:08:14,601 --> 00:08:19,100
and minus seven point seven degrees
per kilometre: colder going up.

123
00:08:19,450 --> 00:08:24,925
In the top gap, above thirteen kilometres,
the sign flips to plus one point one:

124
00:08:24,925 --> 00:08:28,415
above the tropopause,
the lid on the weather layer,

125
00:08:28,415 --> 00:08:30,400
the air gets warmer going up.

126
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:36,250
We can also take a partial
derivative of a partial derivative.

127
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:41,345
Let's take the eastward slope,
partial capital T by partial x,

128
00:08:41,345 --> 00:08:44,100
and ask how fast it changes in time.

129
00:08:44,450 --> 00:08:47,886
Or, the other way
round, take the warming rate,

130
00:08:47,886 --> 00:08:53,150
partial capital T by partial small t, and
ask how it changes going east.

131
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:55,960
These sound like different questions,

132
00:08:55,960 --> 00:08:59,350
but for any smooth field
they give the same answer.

133
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:04,700
Let's check with an easy
field, T equals x squared times small t.

134
00:09:05,050 --> 00:09:10,025
The slope in x is two x small t, and
its rate in time is two x.

135
00:09:11,525 --> 00:09:16,350
The rate in time is x squared,
and its slope in x is also two x.

136
00:09:17,850 --> 00:09:22,201
Over Brest, the reanalysis gives
both as one point four times

137
00:09:22,201 --> 00:09:25,900
ten to the minus nine
degrees per metre per second.

138
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:29,650
Here's why they must agree.

139
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,010
Both are built from the same four values,

140
00:09:33,010 --> 00:09:36,241
at the corners of a small
rectangle in x and

141
00:09:36,241 --> 00:09:41,600
small t: top right, minus top left, minus
bottom right, plus bottom left.

142
00:09:41,950 --> 00:09:46,399
At Brest, the four corners give
zero point three seven degrees,

143
00:09:46,399 --> 00:09:49,789
divided by thirty
six point eight kilometres and

144
00:09:49,789 --> 00:09:54,450
by two hours: the same one point four
times ten to the minus nine.

145
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:59,425
Only the order of the subtractions
differs, so the answer can't.

146
00:10:00,225 --> 00:10:04,325
Now let's change everything
at once, but only by a little.

147
00:10:04,675 --> 00:10:09,309
Suppose we take a small step
east, d x, a small step north,

148
00:10:09,309 --> 00:10:13,550
d y, a small step
up, d z, and wait a short time, d t.

149
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:16,500
How much does the temperature change?

150
00:10:16,850 --> 00:10:20,218
Each partial derivative
says how much T changes

151
00:10:20,218 --> 00:10:23,300
for each unit of step
in its own direction.

152
00:10:23,650 --> 00:10:27,800
So we multiply each one by its own
step, and add them up.

153
00:10:28,150 --> 00:10:34,078
The small change, d T, equals partial
capital T by partial x times d x,

154
00:10:34,078 --> 00:10:37,918
plus partial capital T by
partial y times d y,

155
00:10:37,918 --> 00:10:41,759
plus partial capital T by
partial z times d z,

156
00:10:41,759 --> 00:10:46,100
plus partial capital T by partial
small t times d t.

157
00:10:46,450 --> 00:10:49,175
This is called the total differential.

158
00:10:49,525 --> 00:10:51,625
It's like walking on a hillside:

159
00:10:51,625 --> 00:10:55,234
your rise is the slope going
east times your step east,

160
00:10:55,234 --> 00:10:58,450
plus the slope going
north times your step north.

161
00:10:58,800 --> 00:11:03,045
Unlike a real hillside,
our landscape also changes while

162
00:11:03,045 --> 00:11:05,850
we walk, so the time gets a term too.

163
00:11:06,650 --> 00:11:08,800
Let's try it near Brest.

164
00:11:09,150 --> 00:11:12,883
We go ten kilometres east
and twenty kilometres north,

165
00:11:12,883 --> 00:11:15,925
keep the same
height, and wait half an hour.

166
00:11:16,275 --> 00:11:21,441
The east step gives minus three point two
degrees per hundred kilometres,

167
00:11:21,441 --> 00:11:25,475
times ten kilometres:
minus zero point three two degrees.

168
00:11:25,825 --> 00:11:30,453
The north step gives minus two point five
per hundred kilometres,

169
00:11:30,453 --> 00:11:33,800
times twenty kilometres:
minus zero point five.

170
00:11:34,150 --> 00:11:37,962
And the wait gives
plus two point nine degrees per hour,

171
00:11:37,962 --> 00:11:41,025
times half an hour:
plus one point four five.

172
00:11:42,525 --> 00:11:48,050
Adding them up, the temperature changes by
about plus zero point six three degrees.

173
00:11:49,550 --> 00:11:54,050
This works only for small
steps, where the landscape looks flat.

174
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:58,360
We'll see exactly why, and what
goes wrong for big steps,

175
00:11:58,360 --> 00:12:00,375
when we get to Taylor series.

176
00:12:01,175 --> 00:12:03,925
So far we chose the steps ourselves.

177
00:12:04,275 --> 00:12:09,125
Now let's ride along with something
that moves, like a plane or a balloon.

178
00:12:09,475 --> 00:12:16,304
Its position changes with time, so its x,
y and z are themselves functions of time:

179
00:12:16,304 --> 00:12:20,500
capital X of t,
capital Y of t, and capital Z of t.

180
00:12:20,850 --> 00:12:24,723
In a short time d t, it moves east by d X,

181
00:12:24,723 --> 00:12:29,425
which is d X by d t, its eastward
speed, times d t.

182
00:12:29,775 --> 00:12:32,250
The same goes for north and up.

183
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:36,176
Let's put these steps into
the total differential,

184
00:12:36,176 --> 00:12:38,250
and divide everything by d t.

185
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:42,600
That gives the rate the traveller's
own thermometer reads.

186
00:12:42,950 --> 00:12:48,013
d T by d t equals partial
capital T by partial small t,

187
00:12:48,013 --> 00:12:52,892
plus d X by d t times partial
capital T by partial x,

188
00:12:52,892 --> 00:12:57,771
plus d Y by d t times partial
capital T by partial y,

189
00:12:57,771 --> 00:13:02,650
plus d Z by d t times partial
capital T by partial z.

190
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,050
This is the chain rule.

191
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:08,881
In the index notation
of the vectors movie,

192
00:13:08,881 --> 00:13:12,361
the three moving terms
are d X sub j by d t

193
00:13:12,361 --> 00:13:15,842
times partial
capital T by partial x sub j,

194
00:13:15,842 --> 00:13:16,975
with j summed.

195
00:13:17,775 --> 00:13:20,200
Let's read it term by term.

196
00:13:20,550 --> 00:13:25,675
The first term is the change at a fixed
place: the fixed thermometer's rate.

197
00:13:26,025 --> 00:13:29,224
Each of the other terms
is a speed times a slope:

198
00:13:29,224 --> 00:13:32,226
how fast the traveller
moves in one direction,

199
00:13:32,226 --> 00:13:36,600
times how much the temperature
changes per metre in that direction.

200
00:13:36,950 --> 00:13:41,907
Metres per second times degrees
per metre gives degrees per second,

201
00:13:41,907 --> 00:13:44,275
so every term is a rate in time.

202
00:13:44,625 --> 00:13:47,985
Moving into colder places
cools the traveller,

203
00:13:47,985 --> 00:13:51,125
even if nothing changes
at any fixed place.

204
00:13:51,475 --> 00:13:54,705
And the speeds are
the traveller's own, so a car,

205
00:13:54,705 --> 00:13:58,725
a plane and a balloon at the same
place feel different rates.

206
00:13:59,525 --> 00:14:04,000
Let's try the simplest case
first, on an easy, made-up road.

207
00:14:04,350 --> 00:14:09,259
Imagine a frozen map, where
nothing changes at any fixed place,

208
00:14:09,259 --> 00:14:13,000
so partial capital T by partial
small t is zero.

209
00:14:13,350 --> 00:14:16,991
A car drives east at one
hundred kilometres per hour,

210
00:14:16,991 --> 00:14:22,625
and the air gets colder by zero point zero
three degrees for every kilometre east.

211
00:14:22,975 --> 00:14:26,828
The chain rule gives one
hundred kilometres per hour,

212
00:14:26,828 --> 00:14:30,900
times minus zero point zero three
degrees per kilometre.

213
00:14:31,250 --> 00:14:35,550
The kilometres cancel, leaving
minus three degrees per hour.

214
00:14:37,050 --> 00:14:41,396
So the car's dashboard thermometer
falls by three degrees every hour,

215
00:14:41,396 --> 00:14:45,050
though the air at every fixed
place keeps its temperature.

216
00:14:45,850 --> 00:14:48,800
Now a thought experiment on the real map.

217
00:14:49,150 --> 00:14:53,810
Imagine a small plane at that
height over Brest at six in the evening,

218
00:14:53,810 --> 00:14:57,072
flying due east at one
hundred metres per second,

219
00:14:57,072 --> 00:15:00,400
about three hundred and sixty
kilometres per hour.

220
00:15:00,750 --> 00:15:03,675
Let's call its speed east capital U.

221
00:15:04,025 --> 00:15:06,325
What does its thermometer read?

222
00:15:06,675 --> 00:15:11,450
The place itself is warming,
at plus two point nine degrees per hour.

223
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:14,600
But the plane flies into colder air.

224
00:15:14,950 --> 00:15:17,089
One hundred metres per second,

225
00:15:17,089 --> 00:15:21,294
times minus three point two
degrees per hundred kilometres,

226
00:15:21,294 --> 00:15:25,500
is minus zero point zero zero three two
degrees per second.

227
00:15:25,850 --> 00:15:29,373
Times three thousand six
hundred seconds in an hour,

228
00:15:29,373 --> 00:15:32,625
that's minus eleven point five
degrees per hour.

229
00:15:34,125 --> 00:15:35,099
Adding the two,

230
00:15:35,099 --> 00:15:39,773
the plane's thermometer falls at about
eight point six degrees per hour,

231
00:15:39,773 --> 00:15:42,175
if these rates held for a whole hour.

232
00:15:43,675 --> 00:15:48,275
So the plane cools, even though
every place it passes is warming.

233
00:15:49,075 --> 00:15:53,825
Now let's turn the plane round, and fly
due west at the same speed.

234
00:15:54,175 --> 00:15:58,855
It now flies into warmer air, so
the moving term flips its sign,

235
00:15:58,855 --> 00:16:02,000
to plus eleven point five
degrees per hour.

236
00:16:02,350 --> 00:16:04,470
Adding the warming of the place,

237
00:16:04,470 --> 00:16:08,975
its thermometer rises at about
fourteen point four degrees per hour.

238
00:16:10,475 --> 00:16:13,658
Same place, same moment, same
temperature field,

239
00:16:13,658 --> 00:16:16,775
but a different traveller,
so a different rate.

240
00:16:17,125 --> 00:16:22,260
That's the heart of the chain rule:
d T by d t belongs to the traveller,

241
00:16:22,260 --> 00:16:26,825
while partial capital T by partial
small t belongs to the place.

242
00:16:27,625 --> 00:16:30,900
Now a real traveller: the Trappes balloon.

243
00:16:31,250 --> 00:16:32,572
It rose from one and

244
00:16:32,572 --> 00:16:37,397
a half kilometres to five point eight
kilometres in nine hundred seconds,

245
00:16:37,397 --> 00:16:39,050
which is fifteen minutes.

246
00:16:39,400 --> 00:16:41,902
So its upward speed, d Z by d t,

247
00:16:41,902 --> 00:16:47,063
was four point two six kilometres
divided by nine hundred seconds:

248
00:16:47,063 --> 00:16:49,800
four point seven metres per second.

249
00:16:51,300 --> 00:16:53,363
Over the same fifteen minutes,

250
00:16:53,363 --> 00:16:57,075
its thermometer fell by twenty
four point one degrees.

251
00:16:57,425 --> 00:17:01,661
That's minus zero point zero two seven
degrees per second,

252
00:17:01,661 --> 00:17:04,875
or about one point six
degrees every minute.

253
00:17:06,375 --> 00:17:09,050
Which terms of the chain rule make this?

254
00:17:09,850 --> 00:17:11,519
Let's size each term,

255
00:17:11,519 --> 00:17:16,050
writing the balloon's speeds
as u east, v north and w up.

256
00:17:16,400 --> 00:17:18,650
First, the fixed place.

257
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,177
Comparing this balloon with the one
launched twelve hours earlier,

258
00:17:23,177 --> 00:17:26,025
the air at five point eight
kilometres cooled

259
00:17:26,025 --> 00:17:29,000
by about two point six
degrees in twelve hours.

260
00:17:29,350 --> 00:17:34,700
That's only about minus six times ten
to the minus five degrees per second.

261
00:17:35,050 --> 00:17:37,425
Next, the sideways terms.

262
00:17:37,775 --> 00:17:39,972
The balloon drifted with the wind,

263
00:17:39,972 --> 00:17:43,267
about seventeen kilometres
to the north-north-east,

264
00:17:43,267 --> 00:17:46,174
through air whose
temperature changed by less

265
00:17:46,174 --> 00:17:49,275
than one degree per hundred
kilometres sideways.

266
00:17:49,625 --> 00:17:53,553
But it drifted almost along
the lines of equal temperature,

267
00:17:53,553 --> 00:17:56,548
so only a small part
of that gradient counts:

268
00:17:56,548 --> 00:18:00,875
about minus three times ten
to the minus five degrees per second.

269
00:18:01,225 --> 00:18:05,919
Last, the rising term:
w, four point seven metres per second,

270
00:18:05,919 --> 00:18:09,844
times minus five point seven
degrees per kilometre,

271
00:18:09,844 --> 00:18:14,000
is minus zero point zero two seven
degrees per second.

272
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:21,175
So the rising term makes more than ninety
nine percent of what the thermometer saw.

273
00:18:21,525 --> 00:18:26,125
That's why we can read a balloon's
record as the temperature profile,

274
00:18:26,125 --> 00:18:30,525
partial capital T by partial z, even
though the balloon is moving.

275
00:18:31,325 --> 00:18:34,959
There's one very special
traveller: an air parcel,

276
00:18:34,959 --> 00:18:38,375
a small volume of air
that moves with the wind.

277
00:18:38,725 --> 00:18:43,593
For an air parcel, the speeds
in the chain rule are the wind itself,

278
00:18:43,593 --> 00:18:46,600
and its rate gets its own name and symbol:

279
00:18:46,600 --> 00:18:49,750
capital D T by D t, the
material derivative.

280
00:18:50,100 --> 00:18:55,501
Its moving terms together are the
wind, bold u, dotted with the gradient,

281
00:18:55,501 --> 00:18:59,275
grad T: the dot product
from the dot product movie.

282
00:18:59,625 --> 00:19:03,849
Over Brest, the place warmed by
two point nine degrees per hour,

283
00:19:03,849 --> 00:19:08,800
while the wind brought in warmer air
at three point seven degrees per hour.

284
00:19:09,150 --> 00:19:11,943
If the air parcels had
kept their temperature,

285
00:19:11,943 --> 00:19:15,950
the place would have warmed by
three point seven degrees per hour.

286
00:19:16,300 --> 00:19:18,586
It warmed by only two point nine,

287
00:19:18,586 --> 00:19:23,850
so each air parcel itself cooled by about
zero point eight degrees per hour.

288
00:19:25,350 --> 00:19:30,725
We take this apart fully in the material
derivative movie, later in this series.

289
00:19:31,525 --> 00:19:35,825
The chain rule also lets us
swap one variable for another.

290
00:19:36,175 --> 00:19:41,950
Meteorologists often use pressure instead
of height to say how high something is.

291
00:19:42,300 --> 00:19:45,462
Weather balloons have
long reported pressure,

292
00:19:45,462 --> 00:19:48,975
and many of the equations
become simpler that way.

293
00:19:49,325 --> 00:19:54,125
So how do we turn a rate per kilometre
into a rate per hectopascal?

294
00:19:54,475 --> 00:19:58,994
Along the balloon's climb, height
is a function of pressure,

295
00:19:58,994 --> 00:20:04,416
so partial capital T by partial p equals
partial capital T by partial z,

296
00:20:04,416 --> 00:20:06,600
times partial z by partial p.

297
00:20:06,950 --> 00:20:11,274
Between our two levels, the height
rose four thousand two hundred and

298
00:20:11,274 --> 00:20:16,225
sixty two metres, while the pressure fell
three hundred and fifty hectopascals.

299
00:20:16,575 --> 00:20:22,725
So partial z by partial p is minus
twelve point two metres per hectopascal.

300
00:20:24,225 --> 00:20:27,951
Times minus five point seven
degrees per kilometre,

301
00:20:27,951 --> 00:20:32,700
that gives plus zero point zero six nine
degrees per hectopascal.

302
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:38,475
So the air gets warmer
as the pressure rises, going down.

303
00:20:38,825 --> 00:20:43,775
Pressure as a vertical coordinate
has its own movie in this series.

304
00:20:44,575 --> 00:20:48,600
Now a question that matters a lot
in the physics of air.

305
00:20:48,950 --> 00:20:53,825
When is a small change, d of something,
really the change of a quantity?

306
00:20:54,175 --> 00:20:56,150
Think of climbing a hill.

307
00:20:56,500 --> 00:20:59,739
The height you gain
from the car park to the summit,

308
00:20:59,739 --> 00:21:02,169
about a hundred and thirty five metres,

309
00:21:02,169 --> 00:21:04,473
is the same whichever trail you take:

310
00:21:04,473 --> 00:21:07,775
it depends only on where
you start and where you end.

311
00:21:08,125 --> 00:21:10,398
The distance you walk is different:

312
00:21:10,398 --> 00:21:14,750
four kilometres on the straight
trail, and nine on the winding one.

313
00:21:15,100 --> 00:21:16,917
A quantity like the height,

314
00:21:16,917 --> 00:21:21,225
which depends only on where you
are, is called a state function.

315
00:21:21,575 --> 00:21:26,775
Its small change is called a perfect
differential, or an exact differential.

316
00:21:27,125 --> 00:21:31,700
The temperature, pressure and density
of air are all state functions.

317
00:21:32,050 --> 00:21:34,128
But the distance walked is not:

318
00:21:34,128 --> 00:21:38,150
its small steps add up to a total
that depends on the route.

319
00:21:38,950 --> 00:21:41,950
How can we tell, just from the formula?

320
00:21:42,300 --> 00:21:47,550
Take a small change
of the form M times d x, plus N times d y.

321
00:21:47,900 --> 00:21:50,914
If it's the change of some function f,

322
00:21:50,914 --> 00:21:56,150
then M is partial f by partial x, and N is
partial f by partial y.

323
00:21:56,500 --> 00:21:58,593
And we just saw that mixed partial

324
00:21:58,593 --> 00:22:01,425
derivatives come out
the same in either order.

325
00:22:01,775 --> 00:22:07,875
So the test is: partial M by partial
y must equal partial N by partial x.

326
00:22:08,225 --> 00:22:11,425
Let's try y d x plus x d y.

327
00:22:11,775 --> 00:22:17,350
Partial y by partial y is one, and
partial x by partial x is one.

328
00:22:17,700 --> 00:22:22,225
They match, so it's exact:
it's the change of x times y.

329
00:22:23,725 --> 00:22:26,875
Now try y d x minus x d y.

330
00:22:27,225 --> 00:22:29,675
We get one against minus one.

331
00:22:30,025 --> 00:22:33,475
They don't match, so it's not
the change of anything.

332
00:22:34,975 --> 00:22:39,343
Add it up going once round a square
of side one, anticlockwise,

333
00:22:39,343 --> 00:22:43,225
and you come back to the start
with minus two, not zero.

334
00:22:44,725 --> 00:22:49,850
For a perfect differential, every
round trip adds up to exactly zero.

335
00:22:50,650 --> 00:22:52,375
Now real air.

336
00:22:52,725 --> 00:22:54,666
Its pressure, density and

337
00:22:54,666 --> 00:23:00,025
temperature are tied together by the ideal
gas law: p equals rho R T.

338
00:23:00,375 --> 00:23:03,037
Here p is the pressure, rho the density,

339
00:23:03,037 --> 00:23:06,031
which is the mass of air
in each cubic metre,

340
00:23:06,031 --> 00:23:08,160
and T the temperature in kelvin,

341
00:23:08,160 --> 00:23:12,418
which is the temperature in degrees
Celsius plus two hundred and

342
00:23:12,418 --> 00:23:16,937
seventy three. R is a constant
for dry air: two hundred and

343
00:23:16,937 --> 00:23:20,175
eighty seven joules
per kilogram per kelvin.

344
00:23:20,525 --> 00:23:24,544
So pressure is a function
of density and temperature,

345
00:23:24,544 --> 00:23:29,625
and its total differential
is d p equals R T d rho, plus rho R d T.

346
00:23:29,975 --> 00:23:31,775
Let's test it.

347
00:23:32,125 --> 00:23:38,825
Partial of R T by partial T is R, and
partial of rho R by partial rho is also R.

348
00:23:39,175 --> 00:23:43,925
They match, so pressure passes
the test, as a state function must.

349
00:23:45,425 --> 00:23:49,600
Now divide both
sides by p, which is rho R T.

350
00:23:49,950 --> 00:23:55,275
We get d p over p equals d rho
over rho, plus d T over T.

351
00:23:55,625 --> 00:23:56,233
In words,

352
00:23:56,233 --> 00:24:00,962
the fractional change in pressure
is the fractional change in density,

353
00:24:00,962 --> 00:24:03,800
plus the fractional change in temperature.

354
00:24:04,600 --> 00:24:06,300
Let's use it.

355
00:24:06,650 --> 00:24:11,883
On a sunny afternoon, suppose the air
near the ground warms from two hundred and

356
00:24:11,883 --> 00:24:16,200
ninety to three hundred kelvin, while
the pressure stays the same.

357
00:24:16,550 --> 00:24:21,618
The pressure term is zero, so d rho
over rho equals minus d T over T:

358
00:24:21,618 --> 00:24:27,200
minus ten over two hundred and ninety,
about minus three and a half percent.

359
00:24:28,700 --> 00:24:30,892
So the warmed air is about three and

360
00:24:30,892 --> 00:24:34,850
a half percent lighter than the air
around it, which didn't warm.

361
00:24:35,200 --> 00:24:37,300
That's what makes it rise.

362
00:24:37,650 --> 00:24:42,093
The exact answer is two hundred
and ninety over three hundred,

363
00:24:42,093 --> 00:24:45,175
minus one:
minus three point three percent.

364
00:24:46,675 --> 00:24:47,551
That's close,

365
00:24:47,551 --> 00:24:52,400
because ten kelvin is a small step
compared with two hundred and ninety.

366
00:24:53,200 --> 00:24:55,675
Pressure also changes with height.

367
00:24:56,025 --> 00:24:59,724
In the weather basics movie, we
met hydrostatic balance:

368
00:24:59,724 --> 00:25:04,150
at every height, the pressure holds
up the weight of the air above.

369
00:25:04,500 --> 00:25:08,062
As a differential,
d p equals minus rho g d z,

370
00:25:08,062 --> 00:25:13,250
where g is gravity, nine point eight one
metres per second squared.

371
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:19,100
Going up a small step d z, the pressure
drops by the weight of that thin layer.

372
00:25:19,450 --> 00:25:25,350
Now divide by p, and use the gas
law: rho over p is one over R T.

373
00:25:25,700 --> 00:25:29,852
We get d p over p equals
minus d z over capital H,

374
00:25:29,852 --> 00:25:35,250
where capital H, which
is R T over g, is called the scale height.

375
00:25:36,750 --> 00:25:41,241
At the ground at Trappes,
at nineteen point eight degrees Celsius,

376
00:25:41,241 --> 00:25:43,146
capital H is two hundred and

377
00:25:43,146 --> 00:25:48,725
eighty seven, times two hundred and ninety
three, divided by nine point eight one.

378
00:25:49,075 --> 00:25:53,150
That's about eight thousand
five hundred and seventy metres.

379
00:25:54,650 --> 00:25:59,775
If the temperature were the same all
the way up, capital H would be a constant.

380
00:26:00,125 --> 00:26:04,825
Then d p over p equals
minus d z over capital H says that the

381
00:26:04,825 --> 00:26:09,525
pressure falls by the same
fraction for every metre we climb.

382
00:26:09,875 --> 00:26:14,030
That makes an exponential:
p equals the ground pressure,

383
00:26:14,030 --> 00:26:16,850
times e to the minus z over capital H.

384
00:26:17,200 --> 00:26:20,575
Here e is the number
two point seven one eight.

385
00:26:20,925 --> 00:26:26,730
By the chain rule, its slope, d
p by d z, is minus p over capital H,

386
00:26:26,730 --> 00:26:32,450
so it really does solve d p over p equals
minus d z over capital H.

387
00:26:32,800 --> 00:26:35,968
This is the solution
of a differential equation,

388
00:26:35,968 --> 00:26:40,325
and the ordinary differential
equations movie solves many like it.

389
00:26:40,675 --> 00:26:43,075
Now let's compare with the balloon.

390
00:26:43,425 --> 00:26:47,859
The real pressure follows
the exponential closely near the ground,

391
00:26:47,859 --> 00:26:49,875
then falls below it higher up.

392
00:26:50,225 --> 00:26:53,025
That's because the real air gets colder,

393
00:26:53,025 --> 00:26:56,875
and colder air is denser, so
the pressure falls faster.

394
00:26:57,225 --> 00:27:01,375
We'll use this curve in a minute
to see how Taylor series work.

395
00:27:02,175 --> 00:27:07,450
Now the most important small changes
that are not perfect: heat and work.

396
00:27:07,800 --> 00:27:11,875
They're tied together by the first
law of thermodynamics.

397
00:27:12,225 --> 00:27:15,483
The small change
in the air's internal energy,

398
00:27:15,483 --> 00:27:18,387
d I, the energy of its molecules' motion,

399
00:27:18,387 --> 00:27:24,125
equals a small amount of heat added, plus
a small amount of work done on the air.

400
00:27:24,475 --> 00:27:29,264
We write those two with a d that
has a bar through it, d bar Q and

401
00:27:29,264 --> 00:27:34,125
d bar W, because they're small amounts,
not the change of anything.

402
00:27:34,475 --> 00:27:36,910
Take an air parcel at two hundred and

403
00:27:36,910 --> 00:27:39,740
eighty kelvin, and let
it expand to one and

404
00:27:39,740 --> 00:27:43,425
a half times its volume, ending
at the same temperature.

405
00:27:43,775 --> 00:27:46,773
Route one: first cool
it at a fixed volume,

406
00:27:46,773 --> 00:27:50,050
then heat it as it expands
at a fixed pressure.

407
00:27:50,400 --> 00:27:54,425
Route two: expand it slowly
at a fixed temperature.

408
00:27:54,775 --> 00:27:58,564
Route three: heat it as it
expands at a fixed pressure,

409
00:27:58,564 --> 00:28:00,700
then cool it at a fixed volume.

410
00:28:01,050 --> 00:28:04,720
The heat we must add is twenty
seven, thirty three and

411
00:28:04,720 --> 00:28:07,575
forty kilojoules for each kilogram of air.

412
00:28:09,075 --> 00:28:13,950
Three routes, three different amounts
of heat, between the same two states.

413
00:28:14,300 --> 00:28:16,487
So heat is not a state function:

414
00:28:16,487 --> 00:28:21,475
there's no amount of heat stored
in the air, only heat flowing in or out.

415
00:28:21,825 --> 00:28:23,900
The same is true of work.

416
00:28:24,250 --> 00:28:28,379
But the internal energy, the energy
of the molecules' motion,

417
00:28:28,379 --> 00:28:31,357
changes by exactly zero
on all three routes,

418
00:28:31,357 --> 00:28:35,350
because the parcel ends
at the temperature it started with.

419
00:28:36,150 --> 00:28:38,175
Here's the test at work.

420
00:28:38,525 --> 00:28:42,805
For each kilogram
of air, the heat added in a small,

421
00:28:42,805 --> 00:28:45,850
slow step is c v d T, plus p d alpha.

422
00:28:46,200 --> 00:28:52,800
Here c v is the heat needed to warm one
kilogram by one kelvin at a fixed volume.

423
00:28:53,150 --> 00:28:55,367
Alpha, which is one over rho,

424
00:28:55,367 --> 00:28:59,800
is the volume of one kilogram,
called the specific volume.

425
00:29:00,150 --> 00:29:05,967
That's the first law turned round: c v d
T is the change in the internal energy,

426
00:29:05,967 --> 00:29:11,275
and p d alpha is the work the air does
on its surroundings as it expands.

427
00:29:11,625 --> 00:29:15,200
With the gas law, p is R T over alpha.

428
00:29:15,550 --> 00:29:17,350
Let's test it.

429
00:29:17,700 --> 00:29:20,971
Partial of c v by partial alpha is zero,

430
00:29:20,971 --> 00:29:27,350
but partial of R T over alpha by partial
T is R over alpha, which is not zero.

431
00:29:27,700 --> 00:29:31,650
They don't match, so heat
is not a perfect differential.

432
00:29:33,150 --> 00:29:36,025
Now divide the heat by the temperature.

433
00:29:36,375 --> 00:29:41,150
We get c v d T over T, plus R d alpha
over alpha.

434
00:29:41,500 --> 00:29:44,425
Test again: zero, and zero.

435
00:29:44,775 --> 00:29:46,325
They match!

436
00:29:47,825 --> 00:29:51,738
So heat divided by temperature
is a perfect differential,

437
00:29:51,738 --> 00:29:56,200
the small change of a state function
called entropy, written eta.

438
00:29:56,550 --> 00:30:00,879
On all three of our routes, the entropy
rose by the same amount:

439
00:30:00,879 --> 00:30:04,600
one hundred and sixteen joules
per kilogram per kelvin.

440
00:30:06,100 --> 00:30:08,978
Its close cousin, the
potential temperature,

441
00:30:08,978 --> 00:30:12,183
follows air parcels through
the whole atmosphere,

442
00:30:12,183 --> 00:30:14,800
and it has its own movie in this series.

443
00:30:15,600 --> 00:30:17,525
Now let's zoom in.

444
00:30:17,875 --> 00:30:21,360
Here's the temperature profile
from the Trappes balloon,

445
00:30:21,360 --> 00:30:23,850
all the way up to thirty two kilometres.

446
00:30:24,200 --> 00:30:27,650
From afar, it bends, and it
even has kinks.

447
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:31,475
Let's zoom in around one
and a half kilometres.

448
00:30:31,825 --> 00:30:33,300
And again.

449
00:30:33,650 --> 00:30:35,125
And again.

450
00:30:35,475 --> 00:30:39,575
The closer we look, the straighter
it gets, until it's a line.

451
00:30:39,925 --> 00:30:44,450
That line is the tangent,
and its slope is the derivative there.

452
00:30:44,800 --> 00:30:49,625
So close to any one point, a smooth
curve is almost a straight line.

453
00:30:49,975 --> 00:30:52,950
That's the idea behind Taylor series.

454
00:30:53,750 --> 00:30:56,450
Let's write that line as a formula.

455
00:30:56,800 --> 00:30:58,725
Starting at a height z nought,

456
00:30:58,725 --> 00:31:03,539
the temperature a small step h higher
is about the temperature at z nought,

457
00:31:03,539 --> 00:31:05,400
plus h times the slope there.

458
00:31:05,750 --> 00:31:07,610
At one and a half kilometres,

459
00:31:07,610 --> 00:31:10,625
the temperature was
twelve point three degrees.

460
00:31:10,975 --> 00:31:14,659
The slope, from the readings
just below and just above,

461
00:31:14,659 --> 00:31:17,875
was minus four point nine
degrees per kilometre.

462
00:31:18,225 --> 00:31:21,725
Let's predict the temperature
one kilometre higher.

463
00:31:22,075 --> 00:31:27,065
Twelve point three, plus one
kilometre times minus four point nine,

464
00:31:27,065 --> 00:31:29,150
is seven point four degrees.

465
00:31:30,650 --> 00:31:36,100
The balloon measured six point nine, so
the line is off by only half a degree.

466
00:31:37,600 --> 00:31:41,375
Now let's predict four point two six
kilometres higher.

467
00:31:41,725 --> 00:31:44,273
The line gives minus eight point four,

468
00:31:44,273 --> 00:31:47,625
but the balloon measured
minus eleven point eight.

469
00:31:47,975 --> 00:31:50,625
That's off by more than three degrees.

470
00:31:52,125 --> 00:31:54,871
The farther we go from the starting point,

471
00:31:54,871 --> 00:31:58,075
the worse the line gets,
because the curve bends.

472
00:31:58,875 --> 00:32:02,050
To do better, we add a term for the bend.

473
00:32:02,400 --> 00:32:05,706
The bend is measured by
the second derivative:

474
00:32:05,706 --> 00:32:08,150
how fast the slope itself changes.

475
00:32:08,500 --> 00:32:13,225
We add h squared, times the second
derivative, divided by two.

476
00:32:13,575 --> 00:32:15,700
Why divided by two?

477
00:32:16,050 --> 00:32:19,725
Take the simplest bend, c times h squared.

478
00:32:20,075 --> 00:32:24,525
Its slope is two c h, and its second
derivative is two c.

479
00:32:24,875 --> 00:32:30,250
So to make the bend's second derivative
come out right, c must be half of it.

480
00:32:31,750 --> 00:32:37,050
The same idea gives the next term:
h cubed, times the third derivative,

481
00:32:37,050 --> 00:32:42,350
divided by three times two times
one, which is written three factorial.

482
00:32:42,700 --> 00:32:47,425
Each term makes one more derivative
come out right at the starting point.

483
00:32:47,775 --> 00:32:51,250
Added up for ever, this
is the Taylor series.

484
00:32:51,600 --> 00:32:55,786
f at x nought
plus h equals f, plus h times the slope,

485
00:32:55,786 --> 00:33:00,593
plus h squared over two
factorial times the second derivative,

486
00:33:00,593 --> 00:33:06,175
plus h cubed over three factorial
times the third derivative, and so on.

487
00:33:06,975 --> 00:33:11,800
Let's watch a Taylor series at work
on an easy function, e to the x.

488
00:33:12,150 --> 00:33:17,000
All its derivatives are e to the x too, so
at zero they're all one.

489
00:33:17,350 --> 00:33:22,006
Then e to the h is
one, plus h, plus h squared over two,

490
00:33:22,006 --> 00:33:24,750
plus h cubed over six, and so on.

491
00:33:25,100 --> 00:33:30,258
For h equals zero point one, the sums
are one, then one point one,

492
00:33:30,258 --> 00:33:35,650
then one point one zero five, then
one point one zero five one seven.

493
00:33:37,150 --> 00:33:41,640
The calculator also says
one point one zero five one seven,

494
00:33:41,640 --> 00:33:45,750
so just three terms already
give four correct figures.

495
00:33:47,250 --> 00:33:50,252
For h equals one, we need more terms: one,

496
00:33:50,252 --> 00:33:53,468
two, two point five, two
point six six seven,

497
00:33:53,468 --> 00:33:58,400
two point seven zero eight, and on
towards two point seven one eight.

498
00:33:59,900 --> 00:34:04,275
So a small step needs few
terms, and a big step needs many.

499
00:34:05,075 --> 00:34:06,800
Now the pressure.

500
00:34:07,150 --> 00:34:13,030
Let's expand p equals the ground pressure
times e to the minus z over capital H,

501
00:34:13,030 --> 00:34:15,750
for a height step z above the ground.

502
00:34:16,100 --> 00:34:19,300
The first two terms are
the ground pressure,

503
00:34:19,300 --> 00:34:21,700
times one minus z over capital H.

504
00:34:22,050 --> 00:34:27,315
Look at the slope of that line: the ground
pressure over capital H is rho g,

505
00:34:27,315 --> 00:34:31,125
so this line is just hydrostatic
balance at the ground.

506
00:34:32,625 --> 00:34:37,384
At Trappes, that's a fall of eleven
point six pascals for every metre,

507
00:34:37,384 --> 00:34:41,600
or one hectopascal for every
eight point six metres you climb.

508
00:34:43,100 --> 00:34:46,442
Pilots use this rule
of thumb near the ground:

509
00:34:46,442 --> 00:34:49,275
about thirty feet for each hectopascal.

510
00:34:49,625 --> 00:34:53,150
Let's test it one kilometre
above the station.

511
00:34:53,500 --> 00:34:58,075
The line gives eight hundred
and eighty point four hectopascals.

512
00:34:58,425 --> 00:35:02,635
Adding the bend gives eight hundred
and eighty seven point three,

513
00:35:02,635 --> 00:35:06,975
and the balloon measured eight
hundred and eighty seven point zero.

514
00:35:08,475 --> 00:35:12,335
Now let's go five point six
kilometres above the station,

515
00:35:12,335 --> 00:35:15,925
where the balloon measured
five hundred hectopascals.

516
00:35:16,275 --> 00:35:20,725
The line gives only three hundred
and forty three, far too low.

517
00:35:22,225 --> 00:35:25,741
Adding the bend gives five
hundred and fifty eight,

518
00:35:25,741 --> 00:35:29,050
and the next term gives
five hundred and eleven.

519
00:35:30,550 --> 00:35:34,617
With all the terms, the series
lands on five hundred and

520
00:35:34,617 --> 00:35:37,450
seventeen, the exponential's own value.

521
00:35:38,950 --> 00:35:42,896
The last seventeen hectopascals
are not the series' fault:

522
00:35:42,896 --> 00:35:47,250
our exponential assumed the ground's
temperature all the way up.

523
00:35:47,600 --> 00:35:52,450
With the layer's average temperature,
two hundred and seventy seven kelvin,

524
00:35:52,450 --> 00:35:55,749
the exponential gives four
hundred and ninety nine,

525
00:35:55,749 --> 00:35:58,400
right next to the balloon's five hundred.

526
00:35:59,900 --> 00:36:04,475
So a Taylor series can only be
as good as the curve it expands.

527
00:36:05,275 --> 00:36:07,900
Taylor series need a smooth curve.

528
00:36:08,250 --> 00:36:13,324
At about thirteen kilometres, the
balloon's temperature profile has a kink:

529
00:36:13,324 --> 00:36:17,450
the cooling stops, and above it
the air gets a little warmer.

530
00:36:17,800 --> 00:36:21,475
That's the tropopause,
the lid on the weather layer.

531
00:36:21,825 --> 00:36:27,424
A line or a series built just below the
kink predicts ever colder air above it,

532
00:36:27,424 --> 00:36:28,700
and gets it wrong.

533
00:36:29,050 --> 00:36:31,600
The same happens across a weather front,

534
00:36:31,600 --> 00:36:35,425
where the temperature changes
sharply over a short distance.

535
00:36:35,775 --> 00:36:39,617
So the rule is: use a Taylor
series only for steps that are

536
00:36:39,617 --> 00:36:42,465
small compared
with the distance over which

537
00:36:42,465 --> 00:36:43,525
the curve bends.

538
00:36:44,325 --> 00:36:47,175
So why are these tools
built the way they are?

539
00:36:47,525 --> 00:36:49,700
There are three good reasons.

540
00:36:50,050 --> 00:36:53,955
The first is that the weather
depends on many things at once,

541
00:36:53,955 --> 00:36:55,427
the place and the time,

542
00:36:55,427 --> 00:36:59,461
and partial derivatives let us
ask about one of them at a time,

543
00:36:59,461 --> 00:37:01,125
by holding the rest fixed.

544
00:37:01,475 --> 00:37:07,020
The second is that, when we zoom in close
enough, every smooth field looks flat,

545
00:37:07,020 --> 00:37:12,150
so small changes simply add up, slope
times step, one direction at a time.

546
00:37:12,500 --> 00:37:15,975
That's the total differential,
and the chain rule.

547
00:37:16,325 --> 00:37:18,750
The third reason is practical.

548
00:37:19,100 --> 00:37:21,829
The Taylor series tells
us how big our error

549
00:37:21,829 --> 00:37:24,559
is when we stop
at the straight line, and so

550
00:37:24,559 --> 00:37:26,110
how small a step must be,

551
00:37:26,110 --> 00:37:30,700
whether it's a degree of warming
or the spacing of a weather model's grid.

552
00:37:31,500 --> 00:37:35,052
Keeping only the first two
terms of a Taylor series,

553
00:37:35,052 --> 00:37:38,400
the value and the slope, is
called linearisation.

554
00:37:38,750 --> 00:37:43,509
It turns a curve into a straight
line around a chosen starting state,

555
00:37:43,509 --> 00:37:44,750
for small changes.

556
00:37:45,100 --> 00:37:49,119
Meteorologists and oceanographers
do this all the time:

557
00:37:49,119 --> 00:37:52,700
a quiet starting
state, plus a small disturbance.

558
00:37:53,050 --> 00:37:57,369
That's how waves, and the growth
of storms, are first understood,

559
00:37:57,369 --> 00:38:00,625
in the waves and stability
movies of this series.

560
00:38:00,975 --> 00:38:04,400
Now let's linearise two
famous climate curves.

561
00:38:05,200 --> 00:38:07,475
First, water vapour.

562
00:38:07,825 --> 00:38:13,403
Over a lake or the sea, water molecules
keep escaping into the air as vapour,

563
00:38:13,403 --> 00:38:16,300
a gas, while others return to the water.

564
00:38:16,650 --> 00:38:20,925
The vapour's share of the air pressure
is called the vapour pressure.

565
00:38:21,275 --> 00:38:25,822
When escaping and returning balance,
we say the air is saturated,

566
00:38:25,822 --> 00:38:30,300
and the vapour pressure takes
its saturation value, written e s.

567
00:38:30,650 --> 00:38:34,775
That value depends only
on the temperature, not on the air,

568
00:38:34,775 --> 00:38:38,200
and the Clausius–Clapeyron
equation tells us how.

569
00:38:38,550 --> 00:38:44,975
d e s by d T equals L times e s,
divided by R v times T squared.

570
00:38:45,325 --> 00:38:47,098
Here L is the latent heat,

571
00:38:47,098 --> 00:38:51,666
the energy needed to turn one
kilogram of liquid water into vapour:

572
00:38:51,666 --> 00:38:55,211
about two and a half million
joules at zero degrees,

573
00:38:55,211 --> 00:38:57,325
a little less for warmer water.

574
00:38:57,675 --> 00:39:00,698
R v is the gas constant for water vapour,

575
00:39:00,698 --> 00:39:04,975
four hundred and sixty two
joules per kilogram per kelvin.

576
00:39:05,325 --> 00:39:08,350
And T is the temperature in kelvin.

577
00:39:08,700 --> 00:39:11,401
The curve climbs faster and faster:

578
00:39:11,401 --> 00:39:15,644
at zero degrees Celsius,
e s is about six hectopascals,

579
00:39:15,644 --> 00:39:21,200
at fifteen degrees it's seventeen,
and at thirty degrees it's forty two.

580
00:39:21,550 --> 00:39:25,150
Water in the air has its own
movie in this series.

581
00:39:25,950 --> 00:39:29,250
How much does e s grow
for each extra degree?

582
00:39:29,600 --> 00:39:32,450
Let's divide both sides by e s.

583
00:39:32,800 --> 00:39:37,550
The fractional growth per kelvin
is L over R v T squared.

584
00:39:37,900 --> 00:39:41,638
At zero degrees Celsius,
that's two and a half million,

585
00:39:41,638 --> 00:39:45,648
divided by four hundred and sixty
two times two hundred and

586
00:39:45,648 --> 00:39:49,114
seventy three squared:
zero point zero seven three,

587
00:39:49,114 --> 00:39:51,425
or about seven percent per degree.

588
00:39:52,925 --> 00:39:56,348
At fifteen degrees,
it's six point four percent,

589
00:39:56,348 --> 00:39:59,200
and at thirty degrees, five point seven.

590
00:40:00,700 --> 00:40:05,133
That's the famous seven percent rule:
for each degree of warming,

591
00:40:05,133 --> 00:40:09,225
the saturation vapour pressure
rises by about seven percent.

592
00:40:09,575 --> 00:40:11,525
How good is the line?

593
00:40:11,875 --> 00:40:15,456
For one degree, it gives
seven point three percent,

594
00:40:15,456 --> 00:40:18,475
and the exact curve
gives seven point five.

595
00:40:18,825 --> 00:40:22,512
For four degrees, the line
gives twenty nine percent,

596
00:40:22,512 --> 00:40:25,225
and the exact curve gives thirty three.

597
00:40:26,725 --> 00:40:31,023
The gap grows with the step, just
as the Taylor series told us,

598
00:40:31,023 --> 00:40:33,275
because the bend term is missing.

599
00:40:34,075 --> 00:40:35,975
Why does this matter?

600
00:40:36,325 --> 00:40:39,425
The last ten years were
about one point two six

601
00:40:39,425 --> 00:40:42,525
degrees warmer than
the late eighteen hundreds.

602
00:40:42,875 --> 00:40:44,754
By the seven percent rule,

603
00:40:44,754 --> 00:40:49,450
that raises the saturation vapour
pressure by about nine percent.

604
00:40:50,950 --> 00:40:53,488
The rule is about saturated air, though,

605
00:40:53,488 --> 00:40:57,930
and the water vapour measured near
the ground has risen a little less:

606
00:40:57,930 --> 00:41:01,800
about four to five percent
for each degree of global warming.

607
00:41:02,150 --> 00:41:05,752
Still, the reanalysis shows
the water vapour in the

608
00:41:05,752 --> 00:41:09,425
whole atmosphere rising
since nineteen seventy nine.

609
00:41:09,775 --> 00:41:13,900
More vapour means more water
for the heaviest downpours.

610
00:41:14,250 --> 00:41:19,119
The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate
Change assesses that, worldwide,

611
00:41:19,119 --> 00:41:22,891
the heaviest daily rainfall
is expected to become about

612
00:41:22,891 --> 00:41:27,075
seven percent more intense
for each degree of global warming.

613
00:41:27,875 --> 00:41:30,950
Second, the heat that
Earth sends to space.

614
00:41:31,300 --> 00:41:35,572
Every warm body glows in infrared
light, and a perfect emitter,

615
00:41:35,572 --> 00:41:40,725
called a black body, gives off sigma T to
the fourth watts per square metre.

616
00:41:41,075 --> 00:41:44,410
Here sigma
is the Stefan–Boltzmann constant,

617
00:41:44,410 --> 00:41:48,200
five point six seven times ten
to the minus eight.

618
00:41:48,550 --> 00:41:52,345
Satellites measure Earth giving
off about two hundred and

619
00:41:52,345 --> 00:41:55,075
forty watts per square metre, on average.

620
00:41:55,425 --> 00:41:59,108
Solving sigma T to the fourth
equals two hundred and

621
00:41:59,108 --> 00:42:01,658
forty gives T equals two hundred and

622
00:42:01,658 --> 00:42:05,625
fifty five kelvin, about
minus eighteen degrees Celsius.

623
00:42:05,975 --> 00:42:10,677
That's Earth's emission temperature:
the temperature a black body would need,

624
00:42:10,677 --> 00:42:14,525
to give off those two hundred
and forty watts per square metre.

625
00:42:16,025 --> 00:42:21,150
Now let's linearise: how much more does
Earth give off for each degree warmer?

626
00:42:21,500 --> 00:42:23,691
Just as x squared gave two x,

627
00:42:23,691 --> 00:42:28,375
the derivative of sigma T to the fourth
is four sigma T cubed.

628
00:42:28,725 --> 00:42:33,650
And sigma T cubed is just
sigma T to the fourth, divided by T.

629
00:42:34,000 --> 00:42:38,857
So the slope is four times two hundred
and forty, divided by two hundred and

630
00:42:38,857 --> 00:42:43,650
fifty five: about three point eight
watts per square metre for each degree.

631
00:42:45,150 --> 00:42:49,594
This is called the Planck response,
and it's the planet's thermostat:

632
00:42:49,594 --> 00:42:53,200
warm up, and you lose heat
faster, which pulls you back.

633
00:42:53,550 --> 00:42:56,772
Doubling the carbon dioxide
in the air adds about

634
00:42:56,772 --> 00:43:00,125
three point nine watts
per square metre of heating.

635
00:43:00,475 --> 00:43:05,016
To shed that, a planet with only
the Planck response would warm by

636
00:43:05,016 --> 00:43:08,650
about three point nine
divided by three point eight:

637
00:43:08,650 --> 00:43:11,025
about one point zero four degrees.

638
00:43:12,525 --> 00:43:15,574
The Intergovernmental
Panel's careful estimate

639
00:43:15,574 --> 00:43:18,624
of the real Planck
response is a little lower,

640
00:43:18,624 --> 00:43:22,800
about three point two, which
gives about one point two degrees.

641
00:43:23,150 --> 00:43:27,529
The full warming is larger,
because of feedbacks: for example,

642
00:43:27,529 --> 00:43:33,250
a warmer world has more water vapour in
its air, and water vapour traps heat too.

643
00:43:33,600 --> 00:43:36,275
Is the linear Planck rule good enough?

644
00:43:36,625 --> 00:43:40,916
For one degree, the exact curve
gives three point seven nine,

645
00:43:40,916 --> 00:43:43,800
against the line's three point seven six.

646
00:43:44,150 --> 00:43:47,675
For ten degrees,
it's forty against thirty eight.

647
00:43:49,175 --> 00:43:54,500
For the changes that climate scientists
worry about, the line does very well.

648
00:43:55,300 --> 00:43:58,950
Last, how does a weather
model take a derivative?

649
00:43:59,300 --> 00:44:04,050
It knows the temperature only
at grid points, a distance h apart.

650
00:44:04,400 --> 00:44:08,800
The simplest estimate of the slope
is the forward difference:

651
00:44:08,800 --> 00:44:13,200
the value one step ahead, minus
the value here, divided by h.

652
00:44:13,550 --> 00:44:15,275
How wrong is it?

653
00:44:15,625 --> 00:44:17,850
The Taylor series tells us.

654
00:44:18,200 --> 00:44:22,227
The value one step ahead
is f, plus h times the slope,

655
00:44:22,227 --> 00:44:26,925
plus h squared over two times the second
derivative, and so on.

656
00:44:27,275 --> 00:44:30,550
Let's subtract f, and divide by h.

657
00:44:30,900 --> 00:44:36,775
We get the slope, plus h over two times
the second derivative, plus smaller terms.

658
00:44:37,125 --> 00:44:41,775
So the forward difference
is off by about h over two times the bend.

659
00:44:42,125 --> 00:44:44,975
Halve the step, and you halve the error.

660
00:44:46,475 --> 00:44:49,025
That's called first-order accurate.

661
00:44:49,825 --> 00:44:55,166
Now the centred difference we used
on the map: the value one step ahead,

662
00:44:55,166 --> 00:44:58,875
minus the value one step
behind, divided by two h.

663
00:44:59,225 --> 00:45:01,925
Let's write both with the Taylor series.

664
00:45:02,275 --> 00:45:08,051
Ahead is f, plus h times the slope, plus h
squared over two times the bend,

665
00:45:08,051 --> 00:45:11,825
plus h cubed over six
times the third derivative.

666
00:45:12,175 --> 00:45:16,875
Behind is the same, but every
odd power of h flips its sign.

667
00:45:17,225 --> 00:45:21,825
Subtract, and the f terms
cancel, and so do the bend terms.

668
00:45:22,175 --> 00:45:25,556
Divide by two h, and we're
left with the slope,

669
00:45:25,556 --> 00:45:29,225
plus h squared over six
times the third derivative.

670
00:45:30,725 --> 00:45:34,225
Halve the step, and the error
drops to a quarter.

671
00:45:34,575 --> 00:45:36,875
That's second-order accurate.

672
00:45:38,375 --> 00:45:43,800
Let's check on the sine curve at x equals
one, with a step of zero point one.

673
00:45:44,150 --> 00:45:48,931
The forward difference is off by
minus zero point zero four three,

674
00:45:48,931 --> 00:45:53,350
the centred one by only
minus zero point zero zero zero nine.

675
00:45:54,850 --> 00:45:57,100
Now the real map at Brest.

676
00:45:57,450 --> 00:46:01,194
With the data points eighteen
kilometres apart going east,

677
00:46:01,194 --> 00:46:04,163
the forward difference
gives minus three point

678
00:46:04,163 --> 00:46:06,810
three two degrees per hundred kilometres,

679
00:46:06,810 --> 00:46:10,167
the backward difference
minus three point zero nine,

680
00:46:10,167 --> 00:46:13,975
and the centred one
minus three point two zero, in between.

681
00:46:15,475 --> 00:46:18,275
Now let's pretend the grid is coarser.

682
00:46:18,625 --> 00:46:21,468
With points thirty seven kilometres apart,

683
00:46:21,468 --> 00:46:25,327
the centred difference gives
minus three point one seven,

684
00:46:25,327 --> 00:46:29,525
and seventy four kilometres
apart, minus three point one four.

685
00:46:31,025 --> 00:46:34,077
But at one hundred
and forty seven kilometres,

686
00:46:34,077 --> 00:46:36,532
it drops to minus two point two four,

687
00:46:36,532 --> 00:46:39,518
and at two hundred
and twenty one kilometres,

688
00:46:39,518 --> 00:46:41,575
to minus zero point nine eight.

689
00:46:43,075 --> 00:46:46,186
The coarse grid steps
right over the warm front,

690
00:46:46,186 --> 00:46:49,750
so it sees a temperature
change three times too gentle.

691
00:46:50,100 --> 00:46:53,200
That's why weather models use fine grids.

692
00:46:53,550 --> 00:46:57,440
The first computer weather
forecast, in nineteen fifty,

693
00:46:57,440 --> 00:47:01,825
had grid points seven hundred
and thirty six kilometres apart.

694
00:47:02,175 --> 00:47:06,875
Early climate models had points a few
hundred kilometres apart.

695
00:47:07,225 --> 00:47:10,481
The leading global
forecast models today have

696
00:47:10,481 --> 00:47:13,375
grid points about nine kilometres apart.

697
00:47:13,725 --> 00:47:18,325
Solving whole equations on grids
has its own movie in this series.

698
00:47:19,125 --> 00:47:24,750
To sum up, a derivative is the slope
of the tangent, the rate at one instant.

699
00:47:25,100 --> 00:47:30,075
A partial derivative changes one
variable and holds the others fixed.

700
00:47:30,425 --> 00:47:34,087
Over Brest, the air got
colder by three point two degrees

701
00:47:34,087 --> 00:47:36,703
per hundred kilometres towards the east,

702
00:47:36,703 --> 00:47:41,150
while a fixed thermometer
warmed by two point nine degrees per hour.

703
00:47:41,500 --> 00:47:45,775
The total differential adds
slope times step for each variable,

704
00:47:45,775 --> 00:47:50,525
and dividing by time gives the chain
rule, the rate a traveller feels.

705
00:47:50,875 --> 00:47:55,146
The plane flying east cooled
at eight point six degrees per hour,

706
00:47:55,146 --> 00:47:57,775
while every place it passed was warming.

707
00:47:58,125 --> 00:48:02,447
The Trappes balloon cooled by
one point six degrees every minute,

708
00:48:02,447 --> 00:48:04,375
almost all of it from rising.

709
00:48:04,725 --> 00:48:07,343
Mixed partials don't depend on the order,

710
00:48:07,343 --> 00:48:10,600
and that gives the test
for a perfect differential.

711
00:48:10,950 --> 00:48:16,775
Pressure passes it, heat fails it, and
heat divided by temperature passes again.

712
00:48:17,125 --> 00:48:22,350
A Taylor series builds a curve
from its value, slope, bend and more.

713
00:48:22,700 --> 00:48:25,656
Keeping the first two
terms is linearisation:

714
00:48:25,656 --> 00:48:29,071
about seven percent more
saturation vapour pressure,

715
00:48:29,071 --> 00:48:33,274
and three point eight more watts
per square metre sent to space,

716
00:48:33,274 --> 00:48:34,325
for each degree.

717
00:48:34,675 --> 00:48:37,303
And the same Taylor series tells us how

718
00:48:37,303 --> 00:48:40,200
accurate a weather
model's derivatives are.

719
00:48:41,000 --> 00:48:45,158
This video is one of a series
of related maths tutorials,

720
00:48:45,158 --> 00:48:47,200
so watch out for the others.

721
00:48:47,550 --> 00:48:51,947
They're made for students
of atmospheric science, meteorology and

722
00:48:51,947 --> 00:48:55,600
fluid dynamics, and anyone
else who needs these ideas.

723
00:48:55,950 --> 00:48:58,866
The notebook for derivatives
and Taylor series,

724
00:48:58,866 --> 00:49:01,783
and the notebooks
for the other maths concepts,

725
00:49:01,783 --> 00:49:04,575
are on the website that
goes with this video.
